**Read:** [Physical chemistry of interfaces and colloids — Diethelm Johannsmann](../sources/Physical-Chemistry-of-Interfaces-and-Colloids-Johannsmann.lingua#r_5FAT1MRFSVXVDS9Z6VCF4YM7TD), section 11's drying discussion; [Interfacial Phenomena — John W. M. Bush, MIT 18.357](../../../physics/fluid-dynamics/sources/Interfacial-Phenomena-MIT-18-357.lingua#r_1NGF93FPCCP3FKJS0RAMN929VN), contact lines and interfacial flow; the drying and microstructure studies in [Cleaning technology in semiconductor device manufacturing VIII — ECS proceedings](../sources/Cleaning-Technology-in-Semiconductor-Device-Manufacturing-VIII.lingua#r_1CVF9XCJR7E3J37X5150BDS5Y2).

A receding or pinned liquid boundary redistributes dissolved or suspended material. Flow toward an evaporating pinned edge can concentrate material there; diffusion, surface-tension gradients and moving contact lines can change the result. An average residue amount does not determine its local pattern.

Compare transport time L/U with diffusion time L²/D through Pe=UL/D. This compares mechanisms under a stated geometry; it does not alone predict a ring or a uniform film. Menisci in a narrow gap also exert capillary forces that can bend or attach fine structures during liquid removal.

**Work:** Choose a drying geometry and identify evaporation, liquid flow, diffusion and contact-line motion. Check the units and limiting behavior of Pe. Develop separate observations of residue distribution and structural integrity. Explain why avoiding a liquid–vapor interface may address one capillary mechanism while leaving chemical compatibility and final solvent removal unresolved.

